# Why Carve writes `/italic/` and `*bold*`

The first thing a Markdown user notices about Carve is also the thing most
likely to start an argument:

```carve
/italic/
*bold*
_underline_
~strikethrough~
=highlight=
```

Why spend compatibility on punctuation everyone already knows?

Because familiarity is not the only kind of usability. A syntax can be familiar
because people have memorized it, or understandable because the source itself
suggests what it will do. Carve chose the second kind for inline styling, then
made the boundary rule uniform across all five delimiters.

That trade is worth examining directly.

## Markdown's notation is historical, not inevitable

Markdown inherited email and plain-text conventions rather than designing a
complete visual system. Asterisks and underscores both became emphasis, and a
doubled delimiter became strong emphasis:

```md
*italic*  _also italic_
**bold**  __also bold__
```

This is familiar to millions of developers. It is also redundant: four source
spellings cover two effects, while underline has no standard spelling at all.
Strikethrough usually arrives through GFM as tilde delimiters.

Carve starts from a different question: if each effect had one single-character
delimiter, which character would best hint at the result?

## The source previews the effect

A slash leans forward like italic type. An underscore is a line under the text.
A tilde passes through the vertical middle of lower-case letters. An equals sign
resembles the parallel edges of a marker stroke. The star is the heaviest common
plain-text mark, so it carries bold.

None of these is a perfect miniature rendering. Plain text cannot literally
change weight or draw a line through a word. The goal is a mnemonic strong
enough that a new author can reconstruct the syntax instead of consulting a
cheat sheet.

One character also keeps nested combinations readable:

```carve
/*important aside*/
*bold with /a quieter phrase/ inside*
```

There is no delimiter-run arithmetic and no choice between equivalent
spellings.

## The more important decision is the boundary rule

Choosing attractive punctuation would not help if ordinary text accidentally
triggered it. Carve therefore applies the same word-boundary principle to its
inline delimiters: punctuation inside a word stays punctuation.

```carve
snake_case
/usr/local/bin
key=value
one~two
```

Those examples remain literal text. Formatting begins only where the delimiter
is allowed to open and ends where it is allowed to close.

When formatting inside a word is intentional, braces make the boundary
explicit:

```carve
H{,2,}O
mc{^2^}
x{*very*}large
```

Superscript and subscript are braced-only because caret and comma occur too
naturally in prose to make safe bare delimiters. The inner marks still carry the
mnemonic: the caret points up and the comma hangs below the baseline.

This combination matters more than any one character. A memorable delimiter
without predictable boundaries is a source of accidental markup. A rigorous
boundary rule with arbitrary delimiters is learnable but forgettable. Carve is
trying to get both properties at once.

## Why not keep Markdown aliases too?

Accepting both systems sounds friendly:

```text
*italic* and /italic/
**bold** and *bold*
```

It is not actually possible to give `*text*` both meanings within one inline
dialect. An option could select a compatibility mode, but then the same
delimiter would mean italic in that mode and bold in core Carve.

That is the flavor problem Carve is meant to avoid. Compatibility aliases also
keep parser rules, formatters, documentation, and editor grammars responsible
for spellings the language no longer wants authors to produce.

So Carve makes a clean break: Markdown is an import format, not a second inline
syntax mode. A converter can preserve the meaning of `*italic*` by writing
`/italic/`. Once converted, each Carve styling delimiter has one meaning.

## A migration cost is still a cost

The choice has an obvious downside. Experienced Markdown authors will type
`**bold**` from muscle memory, and existing documents cannot simply be renamed
from `.md` to `.crv`. Search results, keyboards, and editors all reflect
Markdown's installed base.

Carve addresses that cost with conversion and diagnostics rather than pretending
it does not exist:

```sh
carve migrate --from markdown article.md > article.crv
carve lint article.crv
```

The converter works from the parsed Markdown structure, so it can distinguish
emphasis from literal punctuation. The linter flags familiar spellings such as
`**bold**` and `~~strike~~` when they leak into hand-written Carve.

The payoff only makes sense for content that will be edited again. For a frozen
archive, conversion may offer little. For a living documentation set, a CMS, or
a format taught to new authors, the continuing benefit can outweigh the
one-time migration.

## Syntax should optimize for its authors

There is no universally best emphasis notation. A format embedded in GitHub
should optimize for Markdown compatibility. A format intended as a source for
rich, long-lived documents can reasonably optimize for learnability,
predictability, and one meaning per spelling.

Carve's delimiter choice is therefore not “Markdown got italics wrong.” It is a
more specific claim: once a new language has already accepted the compatibility
cost of being new, it should spend that freedom on a coherent system rather
than reproduce every historical choice.

Try the five delimiters in the
[Carve playground](https://markup-carve.github.io/carve/playground), then type
an identifier, a path, and an equation beside them. The interesting question is
not whether the syntax looks unfamiliar for the first minute. It is whether you
still need to look it up ten minutes later.
